-- statement:
--   This block sets up the Parisi variational formula and stochastic-control framework for the ground state of the Sherrington-Kirkpatrick model. standardGaussian is the N(0,1) law on the reals, and gaussianProduct(ι) is the product of standard Gaussians over a finite index type. For N+1 Gaussian coordinates z, coordinate(N,j,z) reads index j mod (N+1). With step h=T/N and mesh times t_j=jh, euler(T,N,γ,u,z) is the Euler scheme starting at 0 with X_{j+1}=X_j+√h·coordinate(N,j,z)+h·γ(t_j)·u(t_j,X_j). An OrderParameter is a function γ on the reals that, on [0,1), is nonnegative, monotone and right-continuous, and is integrable on (0,1]. A BrownianSpace is a probability space carrying a real Brownian motion B indexed by nonnegative reals, with its natural filtration and a shifted filtration generated by increments of B after time t. An admissible control at time t is a progressively measurable process, for the shifted filtration, bounded in absolute value by 1. controlPayoff(t,x,α) is the expectation of |x+B_1−B_t+∫_0^{1−t}γ(t+s)α_s ds| minus (1/2)∫_0^{1−t}γ(t+s)α_s² ds. phi(t,x) is the supremum of this payoff over admissible controls. gradient and curvature are its first and second derivatives in x. parisi(γ) is phi(0,0)−(1/2)∫_0^1 tγ(t)dt, and IsMinimizer(γ) means parisi(γ)≤parisi(η) for every order parameter η. IsDiffusion(γ,X) says X, stopped at time 1, is adapted to the Brownian filtration and, almost surely, X is continuous on [0,1] and satisfies X_t=B_t+∫_0^tγ(s)·gradient(s,X_s)ds. For the Ising model, spin maps true to 1 and false to −1; hamiltonian(n,J,σ)=n^{−1/2}Σ_{i<j}J_{ij}σ_iσ_j; groundStateSequence(n) is (1/n) times the Gaussian expectation, over i.i.d. standard Gaussian couplings J, of the maximum of the Hamiltonian over spin configurations; and groundStateValue is its limit at infinity, defined with limUnder. gradientStopped uses gradient(t,X_t) for t<1 and a terminal variable U at t≥1. ScalarValueConclusion(γ,X) is the defined proposition that the ground-state sequence converges to groundStateValue, and some U exists such that the stopped gradient is a martingale, almost surely |U|≤1, U²=1 and gradient(t,X_t)→U as t→1 from below, E[(gradient(t,X_t)−U)²]→0, groundStateValue=E|X_1|−∫_0^1 tγ(t)dt, groundStateValue=E[U·B_1], and groundStateValue equals ∫_0^1 E[curvature(t,X_t)]dt with that integrand interval integrable on [0,1]. The remaining definitions build a finite Gaussian discretization: eulerPath runs euler with u=gradient; rawCoefficient is curvature at the Euler path and mesh time; normalizer is the inverse root mean square of rawCoefficient; gaussianIncrement is normalizer times rawCoefficient times the coordinate; roundedSign is the sign of gradient(T,X_N)+2·cdf(coordinate N)−1; coefficientA(j) for j≥1 is E[roundedSign·gaussianIncrement(j−1)] and 0 for j=0; coefficientB(i)=E[roundedSign·coordinate(i−1)]; and shiftedCoefficientSum is Σ_{j=0}^{N}A_j·B_{j+1}. FiniteGaussianConclusion(γ,X,T) is the defined proposition that eventually in N all normalizers with j<N are positive, shiftedCoefficientSum tends to ∫_0^T E[curvature(t,X_t)]dt, and these integrals over [0,S] tend to groundStateValue as S→1 from below. The block contains only definitions and these two conclusion propositions, with no theorem asserting them.
